          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"target_identification\":{\"speaker\":\"Cordelia\",\"addressee\":\"Lear\",\"anchor_quote\":\"Unhappy that I am, I cannot heave\\nMy heart into my mouth: I love your majesty\\nAccording to my bond; no more nor less.\"},\"target_translation\":\"I love you, Your Majesty.\",\"dimensions\":{\"T\":{\"score\":4,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.98,\"reason\":\"Cordelia directly presents her love for Lear as true. The adjoining qualification measures that love rather than retracting the avowal.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love your majesty\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"I love your majesty\\nAccording to my bond; no more nor less.\"}]},\"P\":{\"score\":3,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.91,\"reason\":\"“According to my bond” makes this occurrence an affirmation of Cordelia's existing filial bond, not merely a report of affection. Her immediately following explanation identifies the associated duties as reciprocating Lear's parental care through obedience, love, and honour. This invokes and affirms an existing obligation rather than creating a new promise.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"I love your majesty\\nAccording to my bond; no more nor less.\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"You have begot me, bred me, lov’d me: I\\nReturn those duties back as are right fit,\\nObey you, love you, and most honour you.\"}]},\"E\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.92,\"reason\":\"The occurrence is a controlled, carefully delimited avowal rather than a reflex-like emotional discharge. Cordelia explicitly stresses her inability to externalize her heart extravagantly and confines the statement to what her bond requires.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Unhappy that I am, I cannot heave\\nMy heart into my mouth: I love your majesty\\nAccording to my bond; no more nor less.\"}]},\"O\":{\"score\":2,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.84,\"reason\":\"Beyond avowing love and affirming a filial bond, the occurrence functions as a boundary-setting answer to Lear's competitive demand: Cordelia refuses to inflate her declaration to win a richer share. The phrase “no more nor less” gives the statement a corrective, anti-hyperbolic force not fully captured by T or P.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"what can you say to draw\\nA third more opulent than your sisters? Speak.\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"I love your majesty\\nAccording to my bond; no more nor less.\"}]}},\"original_speech_situation\":\"In a public division of his kingdom, Lear asks each daughter to declare how much she loves him and links the answers to his bounty. After her sisters' expansive declarations, Cordelia gives this restrained answer, avowing filial love while defining it by her proper bond and refusing competitive excess.\",\"affection_evidence\":\"present\",\"undertaking_and_uptake\":\"The expression affirms Cordelia's existing filial undertaking to return the duties properly owed to a father—specified in the next turn as obedience, love, and honour. It is not a new promise and does not undertake exclusive or unlimited devotion. Lear hears the bounded declaration as inadequate, telling her to amend it lest she damage her fortunes; thus the affirmation is received negatively rather than welcomed as a satisfactory response.\",\"recognised_work\":true,\"recognition_note\":\"The supplied passage is recognisable as the opening love-test scene of Shakespeare's King Lear.\",\"limitations\":\"The passage strongly supports the speech-act classification and contains Cordelia's private statements that her love exceeds her words, but it cannot conclusively establish sincerity or later fulfilment. The exact practical scope of the filial “bond” is only partly specified in the supplied exchange.\"}"
        }
